This study examines the interaction of neutrinos with photons facilitated by light (pseudo)scalar mediators, a mechanism that enables the detection of diffuse neutrino fluxes through optical signals. We specifically analyze the case of a dark photon with mass $m_A$ and kinetic mixing parameter $\epsilon$ coupled to the Standard Model, as well as a pseudovector mediator $m_\mu$ interacting with neutrinos via Yukawa coupling. The resulting effective operator, $\mathcal{O}_{
u\gamma}$, is derived at the one-loop level, yielding a neutrino-photon coupling $C_{
u\gamma} \propto \sum_i U_{i	au}^* U_{i\mu} f(m_A^2/M_W^2)$, where $U$ represents the PMNS matrix and $f$ is a loop function. We calculate the anticipated photon flux from diffuse neutrino sources, such as the Sun, and evaluate the potential for observing this signal using the HESS and MAGIC telescopes. Our findings indicate that the projected sensitivities of these instruments could probe the neutrino-photon coupling down to $C_{
u\gamma} \sim 10^{-14}$ for mediator masses below 100 MeV, thereby offering a novel experimental avenue to investigate neutrino flavor oscillations.
